Interpretation C — Platonic morphospace is independently real

Levin’s speculative proposal goes further.

Some patterns may not merely be descriptions or implicit physical possibilities. They may belong to a real, structured option space that physical systems can interface with or “sample.”

His argument is roughly:

Mathematical objects have objective properties not created by physical systems.
These properties constrain physical and biological outcomes.
Spaces of possible patterns appear structured rather than arbitrary.
New embodiments can reveal coherent behaviours and forms not explicitly selected or programmed.
It may therefore be scientifically useful to treat patterns as occupants of a real option space rather than as retrospective labels.

Levin is unusually explicit that he does not have a completed metaphysics. He says he is extending Platonism as a research programme, considers mathematical entities the low-agency end of a possible spectrum, and speculates that more complex patterns might possess plasticity or agency. He also acknowledges that the junction between nonphysical pattern space and physical realization remains unresolved.

So he is hinting at:

embodiments may act as interfaces through which
